      "problem": "${(-\\frac{1}{9})}^{2}=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that $-\\frac{1}{9}=\\frac{(-1)}{9}.$\n\nTo raise the fraction to the power of $2$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $2:$\n\n$$\n{(-\\frac{1}{9})}^{2}={(\\frac{(-1)}{9})}^{2}=\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{{9}^{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, using $(-1)^{2}=1$ and ${9}^{2}=81,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{{9}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{81}.\n$$",
